{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789329720,"nonce":"YDzsNEfyIhHqDLq9cJ37Q10Dw0s=","title":"Host natives called by record","message":"A host native's C entry is called with the arguments the program's\nsignature declares, so a host standing one entry behind natives of many\nsignatures needs one C function per signature shape, which is\ncombinatorial. Caribou's Haxe-to-Wren bridge declared its natives with\nevery parameter `Dynamic` for that reason, and Ash boxed every numeric\nargument (`hlp_make_dyn`, an allocation) before each call.\n\nThe stub bridge already packs a call into a record of words for the\ninterpreter (floats of either width as f64 bits, narrow integers in the\nlow bits, pointers as they are). The same record, offered to a host native\nthat asks for it, lets one C entry serve every signature and unpack by\nthe kinds it knows: `HostNative::record`, called as\n`addr(context, args) -\u003e word` by the interpreter, the Cranelift tier and\nthe LLVM tier's caller thunk, with the result read back by the declared\nreturn kind. Ahead-of-time linking refuses it as it refuses a context\nword.\n\nVerified by `ash_interp/tests/host_module.rs`, which adds a record native\ntaking an object, a float and a bool and returning a float, reached from\nevery tier, and by the parity matrix.","files":null}]}